  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 155 PM CDT Mon Aug 17 2026\n\nToday Through Tomorrow:\n\nWater vapor imagery this afternoon shows a belt of ascent \noverspreading the northern High Plains as a shortwave moves into the \nregion while quasi-zonal flow mid/upper flow remains laid over the \neastern half eastern half of the CONUS. We've trended warmer and \ndrier than the initial guess from the NBM this afternoon, which \nlikely has some bias correction from our recent rainy and humid \npattern. \n\nWarm conditions and modest moisture pooling have developed across \nnorth-central Nebraska into South Dakota ahead of a surface trough \nlaid from northwest Nebraska into eastern North Dakota where it \nmeets the main cold front. Further destabilization is expected \nto occur over the course of the afternoon and early evening \nhours, with isolated storm development possible along it shortly\nafter 5 PM before shifting into northeast Nebraska closer to \nmidnight. As it approaches the area, it will grow into an MCS \nthat forecast soundings indicate will be cold pool-driven and \ngusty, with a few hailstones possible as well. Speeds of 60-70 \nmph seem plausible as it moves through, with additional activity\nbuilding behind it off of the nose of the LLJ. As the \nconvection trains over the same areas, flooding will become an \nincreasing threat. PWAT values aren't overly impressive at 1.7\",\nbut much of that area has already seen 4\" or more in the last \n4-5 days. After the initial MCS, that training convection will \nbe capable of marginally severe wind and hail through the \novernight hours as it shifts slowly southward. By sunrise \ntomorrow morning, that shower and storm activity will continue \nto carry marginal severe potential (owing mostly to the better \nshear we've seen over the past couple of months). The evolution \nfrom 6 AM onward is still a bit murky, where the longevity of \nthe activity will limit afternoon potential. Ambient shear \nvalues and instability will be sufficient for supercellular \nstructures, while boundary-parallel shear values will promote \nupscale growth. Southeast Nebraska has a narrow shot at seeing \nthe initial development of the evening storms that could carry \nsome large hailstones, damaging winds, and an isolated tornado. \nIt is more likely that the main boundary will have pushed south \nand they miss out on those chances. Lingering light rain showers\nin the belt of ascent will linger into the overnight hours, as \nwe dip into the low- to-mid 60s.\n\nWednesday and Beyond:\n\nWith the passage of the early week action, things will start \nquieting down heading towards the weekend. Mid/upper ridging will \ntake further hold over the western CONUS, with the area being on the \neastern fringe of it, dodging the warm temperatures. A few \nshortwaves will also round the top of the ridge and move through the \narea, with the most likely one occurring Wednesday afternoon \ninto the evening hours where storms (some severe possible) \ndevelop to the west and waft into eastern Nebraska. Thursday \nonward sees precipitation chances decrease, with temperatures \nthat peak Friday in the mid-to-upper 80s before settling back \ndown in the low 80s for the remainder of the weekend.",
